INSERT INTO deep_dive_pages (country_id, last_updated) SELECT id, '2026-08-05' FROM countries WHERE name_en = 'Hungary';
INSERT INTO deep_dive_page_translations (country_id, lang, compliance_model, footer_disclaimer, timeline_intro, file_format_intro, scope_intro, steps_intro, penalties_intro, mandate_summary, mandate_summary_icon) SELECT id, 'en', 'Real-time transaction-data reporting (RTIR) since 2018, layered with a narrow energy/water B2B e-invoicing mandate since 2025 -- no general B2B e-invoicing mandate yet, though one is under consultation', 'This deep-dive page reflects publicly available NAV guidance and industry reporting as of early August 2026 and is provided for general awareness, not legal or tax advice. Hungary''s comprehensive future e-invoicing framework was still at the public-consultation stage as of this writing, with no enacted date -- confirm current status directly with NAV or a Hungarian tax adviser before relying on this page for compliance decisions.', 'Hungary''s Real-Time Invoice Reporting (RTIR) system is one of the most mature transaction-reporting regimes in Europe: launched 1 July 2018 for domestic B2B invoices above a HUF 100,000 VAT threshold, the threshold was eliminated on 1 July 2020, and scope widened again on 1 January 2021 to cover B2C, export, and intra-Community invoices -- by which point RTIR touched essentially every VAT-relevant invoice a Hungarian business issues. Crucially, RTIR reports invoice DATA to NAV''s Online Szamla system; it has never required the invoice itself to be issued electronically. That changed, narrowly, from 1 July 2025: electricity, natural gas, and water utility suppliers must now issue actual e-invoices for B2B supplies to non-private customers -- Hungary''s first genuine e-invoicing issuance mandate, though sector-specific. From 1 September 2026, the reporting net widens again to receipts. Separately, NAV and the Ministry for National Economy opened a public consultation in November 2025, updated as a concept paper on 31 March 2026, on a comprehensive future e-invoicing framework covering domestic and intra-EU B2B and B2G transactions -- proposed, not yet enacted. Whatever Hungary decides domestically, the EU''s ViDA cross-border floor applies from 1 July 2030 regardless.', 'RTIR transmits invoice data through NAV''s Online Szamla API, typically in NAV''s own XML schema generated directly by invoicing/accounting software; it does not mandate a specific invoice document format, since the invoice itself can remain paper or PDF outside the energy/water sector. Where a real e-invoicing mandate does apply (electricity, gas, and water B2B supplies since July 2025), the legislation doesn''t prescribe a specific format either -- any format and transmission method NAV accepts qualifies. B2G invoicing follows the EN 16931 European Standard, as in every EU member state.', 'RTIR''s data-reporting duty now covers essentially all VAT-registered businesses in Hungary, for domestic B2B, B2C, export, and intra-Community invoices alike -- a scope that took three steps (2018, 2020, 2021) to reach. Layered on top, the July 2025 e-invoicing issuance mandate is deliberately narrow: only electricity, natural gas, and water utility suppliers, only for B2B supplies to non-private customers. From September 2026, all businesses -- not just VAT-registered ones supplying B2B -- come into scope for receipt-data reporting. No general domestic B2B e-invoicing mandate exists yet; the November 2025 consultation''s proposed scope (domestic B2B, intra-EU B2B, and B2G) has no confirmed date.', 'Because RTIR''s reporting duty is already close to universal, most Hungarian businesses'' near-term work is about layering new obligations onto existing RTIR connectivity rather than building compliance from scratch: energy/water suppliers need actual e-invoicing capability, most other businesses need to get ready for receipt-data reporting by September 2026, and everyone should watch the comprehensive e-invoicing consultation for a future domestic B2B mandate.', 'Hungary backs RTIR with real, sourced penalties: up to HUF 500,000 per invoice not reported, reported late, incompletely, incorrectly, or untruthfully, plus a separate HUF 300,000 fine (from 1 January 2025) for failing to respond to a NAV clarification request within 15 days. No penalty schedule specific to the narrower July 2025 energy/water e-invoicing mandate was found in research as of this writing.', 'Hungary''s National Tax and Customs Administration (NAV) has operated Real-Time Invoice Reporting (RTIR) since 1 July 2018, now covering essentially every VAT-relevant invoice a Hungarian business issues -- but RTIR is a transaction-DATA reporting duty, not an e-invoicing format mandate; the underlying invoice can remain paper or PDF. That changed narrowly from 1 July 2025, when electricity, natural gas, and water utility suppliers became required to actually issue electronic invoices for B2B supplies to non-private customers -- Hungary''s first real e-invoicing issuance mandate, though sector-specific rather than economy-wide. From 1 September 2026, mandatory reporting expands again, this time to receipt data. A comprehensive future e-invoicing framework -- covering domestic and intra-EU B2B and B2G transactions -- was opened for public consultation in November 2025 and updated as a concept paper on 31 March 2026, but remains proposed rather than enacted. Regardless of Hungary''s own domestic timeline, the EU''s VAT in the Digital Age (ViDA) directive sets a confirmed cross-border B2B floor from 1 July 2030.', '🇭🇺' FROM countries WHERE name_en = 'Hungary';
